Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||10-17-1999||07:27 PM||||well, can ANYONE at all help me out with these problems? i really need to find a way to get my hood open, my car overheated and i have no way to put coolant in now! is there any way to get to the latch without the cables? is there any way i could un-bolt the hinges and open the hood backwards so then i could unbolt the latch? someone please reply...||||reg||1
Z||000004||RFMike||10-17-1999||07:47 PM||||FieroStetz,<BR>The hood can be opened from the front by removing the facia.  This will give you access to the hinges, and you can remove the hinges, and you're in.  However, if you were hit in the front, it probably drove the hood back and engaged the safety slots.  If that happened, let me know, and I'll go through how to remove the hood.  Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by RFMike (edited 10-18-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero||10-17-1999||08:29 PM||||FieroStetz...are you sure when the hood cable snaps...there's nothing left underneath the dash where you can use a pliers and pull the remaining cable to open the hood...check the routing of the cable to see if you could see any remaining cable...if so use a pliers and pull it...||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroSTETZ||10-18-1999||01:48 PM||||I wasn't hit, someone at my high school jumped on my hood and broke the latch. pulling both cables as hard as i can does nothing... theres no damage, but i fixed a dent on my hood with a suction cup and hot water and voila, no more foot dent. i just need to get the hood open so i can install a new latch, and add coolant to the overflow resevoir. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||10-18-1999||07:31 PM||||Try having someone sit on the hood just above the release latch, and then tug on the release cable(s) and see if that opens her up.  If not, I'd have to say, go with removing the front fascia. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000008||lowCG||10-18-1999||09:46 PM||||Never seen a fiberglass panel dent before,much less have a memory,or allow for any forming.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroSTETZ||10-18-1999||10:09 PM||||weeeeeel, i think the suction cup did all the work, but i saw the hot water trick on some message board about saturns or something.... it wasnt really dented, more of a dimple, know what i am saying? oh, if it would make things easier, i have an '86 SE 2m6, and my headlight covers are open, even though the hood does not open... do either of these help me? i can't quite reach some of the torx screws from the little crack between the hood and front fascia. is there any way to get at the hinges from below the car, or perhaps through the headlight holes?||||reg||1
Z||000010||RFMike||10-18-1999||10:32 PM||||No.  It took me about 25 minutes to pull the facia.  I had a cordless drill with Torx and Phillips bits for it.  The hard part will be getting the hood aligned again.  Removing the hinge mounting bolts will change the placement, and if the bolts haven't left marks on the hinge body, you won't know where to locate them when you tighten it in place.  Everyone of us should scribe the bolt locations on the front and rear decks for this reason.<BR>After my experience in the parking lot from hell, I know how time consuming it can be to get the hood aligned properly, especially if someone has bashed your baby.||||reg||1
